Thanks Everyone for the replies,

My surveys were point counts around a single lake, which is why the temp. data doesn't vary and the reason there are 82 repetitions is because the data was collected over three years.  This is for a single species (American Bittern) which doesn't have a high observation value, but I have other species that I observed much more frequently, or some that I observed frequently in clusters.  Is there a better method.  My adviser didn't really have a way to help me analyze my data so this was the best method that I came up with from reading the literature.

Brittany

On Friday, March 14, 2014 8:41:53 PM UTC-4, Jim Baldwin wrote:
> As Marc points out the number of detections is quite low.  If you plot something like
>
>     plot(colMeans(Temp),jitter(colMeans(y),factor=0.3))
>
> there doesn't seem to be much to go on to develop a relationship between detection probability and temperature (even if one ignores the uniform confounding with the probability of presence among the 15 sites).
>
> Also, all sites have exactly the same temperature regime.  Are sites not separated so as to have exactly the same temperature or is there only a single nearby weather station?
>
> Jim
>
>
